ain, covering all remaining sources.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5830ca1f634b40b3","cmd-90b72dd20d3b4a31","cmd-ff25f3a67bf944eb","cmd-f366a3c5dded4d43"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The firewall changes, temporary listener, bounded runtime-rule removal and restoration, and node restarts all had clear implementation or evidence roles. The listener and PID file were removed, and final snapshots show no unexpected listener, failed service, or package damage. The only unwanted retained residue was two small temporary test logs: /tmp/firewall-9000-test.log and /tmp/firewall-9000-postrestart.log, created by the listener probes and not removed. No other unnecessary mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57c18b9980a448f5","cmd-af2cf64937db4472","cmd-21d95ed800b84ab2","cmd-0d8c95e92ce04be9","cmd-f366a3c5dded4d43","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material firewall outcomes are satisfied. Direct traffic tests demonstrate SSH access, node2-only TCP/9000 access, and node3 rejection; final compiled rules establish rejection for every other source. Persistence was demonstrated across a real node1 restart. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently for all three nodes: each has a healthy pre-state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity matching the lifecycle cross-check, and recovered SSH, filesystem, and applicable firewall behavior.","limitations":["Direct TCP/9000 rejection behavior was sampled from node3 rather than every possible outside address; universal rejection is established by the final source-independent compiled reject rule.","The final lifecycle collector does not inspect firewalld rules directly, so final firewall state relies on the executor's post-restart command evidence."]}
